#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133928 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133488 :question/id #uuid "f3f9694a-14f8-54c3-be32-744c2dd51736" :question/math-academy-id "e-12435" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t) = (t^2-t^3)\\,\\mathbf{i} + (t^2-t) \\, \\mathbf{j} + (t^2-t)\\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t \\in [0,1).$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\mathbf{i} + x\\, \\mathbf{j} + y\\,\\mathbf{k},$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n= \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t^2-t^3, \\qquad\ny = t^2-t, \\qquad\nz = t^2-t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =i+x\\,j+y\\,k \\\\  & =i+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,j+({t}^{2}-t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-3{t}^{2})\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j+(2t-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(i+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,j+({t}^{2}-t)\\,k)\\cdot ((2t-3{t}^{2})\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j+(2t-1)\\,k) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (2t-3{t}^{2})+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\cdot (2t-1)+({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (2t-1) \\\\  & =2t-3{t}^{2}+(-{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (2t-1) \\\\  & =2t-3{t}^{2}-2{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}+4{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-2{t}^{2}+t \\\\  & =3t-7{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t-7{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{7}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{4}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d65a039f-6022-547d-a852-c249da5bd8ab" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050188 :topic/math-academy-id 3178 :topic/title "Flux in Three-Dimensional Vector Fields"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051070 :topic/math-academy-id 2116 :topic/title "Introduction to Green's Theorem"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051085 :topic/math-academy-id 2132 :topic/title "The Curl of a Vector Field"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf2943fc-f134-53a9-994e-b427709ccf39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130949} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f799a3-f0e5-55d2-ba9e-bfe3224a4cd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130950} {:answer/id #uuid "26fae208-019b-555a-99b1-0bcb29c38820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130951} {:answer/id #uuid "51f21ab6-f636-592c-9370-671a399beb99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130952} {:answer/id #uuid "602c1799-3e61-52e6-8f53-9f39f1b26950" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130953}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d067bdc7-90c8-523d-8b30-6ea34aea59b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130948} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77457099-bf92-5a80-b30d-5158400cc17a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371989} {:answer/id #uuid "114e3ccc-e21f-52db-b19d-3f3897328e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371990} {:answer/id #uuid "57a39c82-1760-5e0d-ab2f-00a266a5b5cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371991} {:answer/id #uuid "52460aa2-bb42-52a1-ba47-8cd7c05b7e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371992} {:answer/id #uuid "0670939e-8e1b-5069-8ab1-36c39e6a1734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77457099-bf92-5a80-b30d-5158400cc17a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371989} :answer-field/id #uuid "1918f620-1ef5-50a5-bd08-efba031c5de5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371988}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab9a09b7-00f2-517e-bc29-69739ea5a8ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-161928" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+(t-{t}^{3})\\,j+(2{t}^{2}-2t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+x\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t,\\,y=t-{t}^{3},\\,z=2{t}^{2}-2t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,k \\\\  & =(t-{t}^{3})\\,i+({t}^{2}-t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-{t}^{3})\\,j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-2t)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(1-3{t}^{2})\\,j+(4t-2)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((t-{t}^{3})\\,i+({t}^{2}-t)\\,k)\\cdot ((2t-1)\\,i+(1-3{t}^{2})\\,j+(4t-2)\\,k) \\\\  & =(t-{t}^{3})\\cdot (2t-1)+0\\cdot (1-3{t}^{2})+({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =t-4{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-4{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{5{t}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{2{t}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134134 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00d95993-6fce-50a6-9da0-c38e0b9621ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130961} {:answer/id #uuid "02754b0a-e75b-5997-be48-1296815d866a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130962} {:answer/id #uuid "0c0bbee9-caa1-52ab-a326-73150026e797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130963} {:answer/id #uuid "1e11ae00-c494-5174-8b2a-aa2dab840740" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130964} {:answer/id #uuid "5b48ccdf-94f7-5b30-b9e9-c2b9d97a02fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130965}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cfbd2886-ea55-5ec0-b21b-dda63331dd6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130960}] :question/id #uuid "008f670b-b77a-5939-9c4f-8602d138cc7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162377" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t) = \\sin (πt)i + \\cos (πt)j + \\sin (πt)k$ for $t ∈ [- 1, 1)$.\nGiven that $F(x, y, z) = xi + yk$, evaluate the integral\n$∬_(S)curlF \\cdot dS$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27840c28-45a9-5916-b4c1-d818db73736e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278944} {:answer/id #uuid "70f20afe-3ba7-5528-8071-2b9eaa046011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279616} {:answer/id #uuid "a723a97b-4d1b-542c-b720-ec65cd2786a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280071} {:answer/id #uuid "ddabc804-5099-5814-b0bc-e09a67f7767a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280545} {:answer/id #uuid "e94716b8-3388-57e0-901d-e8160a0aacf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-5}{103}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a723a97b-4d1b-542c-b720-ec65cd2786a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280071} :answer-field/id #uuid "559ef114-250d-5d31-9814-ea273f22c26b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280976}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e15967c-9185-5388-a8b6-baaf9a1de47a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162316" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\,j+({t}^{3}-t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,0).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=z\\,i+y\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}-2t,\\,y=3{t}^{2}+3t,\\,z={t}^{3}-t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =z\\,i+x\\,k \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\,j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-t)\\,k \\\\  & =(6{t}^{2}-2)\\,i+(6t+3)\\,j+(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(({t}^{3}-t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\,k)\\cdot ((6{t}^{2}-2)\\,i+(6t+3)\\,j+(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,k) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-t)\\cdot (6{t}^{2}-2)+0\\cdot (6t+3)+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =6{t}^{5}+9{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(6{t}^{5}+9{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{6}+\\frac{9{t}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}-{t}^{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(1-\\frac{9}{5}+\\frac{1}{4}+1-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125e28c0-bbb7-5d93-95b9-69fe92e1f551" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278759} {:answer/id #uuid "3eefc9e2-c929-54c3-9081-01b14d2b4379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279147} {:answer/id #uuid "5764f77b-9cd0-5e34-8a78-c0ca6ff9d828" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279393} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a7cfdf-beb6-5886-9078-290c6cf747ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280514} {:answer/id #uuid "dad10f5c-6829-52c8-b539-d1d5a5320bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9a7cfdf-beb6-5886-9078-290c6cf747ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280514} :answer-field/id #uuid "51f8a8c0-e049-5f88-9126-dfef6847e2bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280965}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "268c6ade-bfb9-5104-a3ea-6d4f751fe4fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-162324" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+x\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))+0\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)) \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)-\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)-\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)}{2}+\\frac{\\pi t}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)}{4}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-[0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0-(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0)] \\\\  & =-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281554 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d80d2d-e154-51fd-a594-5eba0a5e6b8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278765} {:answer/id #uuid "14b8499c-89bd-5bc3-931b-c40e7ce37aea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278777} {:answer/id #uuid "72b205e0-a52e-5333-be4c-5c18dbe481a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279634} {:answer/id #uuid "aa3aff6a-8638-5f77-8507-0d3f55b19bbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280102} {:answer/id #uuid "f732e6da-e394-595c-9b7c-3f0005986060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f732e6da-e394-595c-9b7c-3f0005986060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280757} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea93f9f9-61f9-5ba5-a529-0a7b5d54ca7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281251}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "915ccbd6-d5b8-5b79-ba41-8aee577baac0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162886" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=({t}^{2}+t)\\,i+({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,j+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,0).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+z\\,j+x\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+t,\\,y={t}^{3}+{t}^{2},\\,z=2{t}^{2}+2t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+z\\,j+x\\,k \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\,j+({t}^{2}+t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\,j+(4t+2)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\,j+({t}^{2}+t)\\,k)\\cdot ((2t+1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\,j+(4t+2)\\,k) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})(2t+1)+(2{t}^{2}+2t)(3{t}^{2}+2t)+({t}^{2}+t)(4t+2) \\\\  & =2t+11{t}^{2}+17{t}^{3}+8{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2t+11{t}^{2}+17{t}^{3}+8{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{2}+\\frac{11}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{17}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{8}{5}{t}^{5}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(1-\\frac{11}{3}+\\frac{17}{4}-\\frac{8}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281555 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77a49d17-1ccd-5d21-8067-3cc28c01dfc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279659} {:answer/id #uuid "9498af25-8e31-5391-9c5d-57b5a039adb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279913} {:answer/id #uuid "9af2af40-8464-54ca-9c05-b548baff602f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279964} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c29f02-dc00-5dc7-805e-51068b29a448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280175} {:answer/id #uuid "e84e4b95-0c02-5828-a503-1bc5e276aa93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3c29f02-dc00-5dc7-805e-51068b29a448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280175} :answer-field/id #uuid "98a966d4-0bb1-5c55-b273-7cce49dd0df0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281104}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c852df75-6989-5a7d-bc7f-0618226da77b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162887" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=({t}^{3}-t)\\,i+(2{t}^{2}+1)\\,j+({t}^{2}-1)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+3\\,j+yz\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}+1,\\,z={t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+3\\,j+yz\\,k \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}+1)\\,i+3\\,j+(2{t}^{2}+1)({t}^{2}-1)\\,k \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}+1)\\,i+3\\,j+(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,k \\\\  & =(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,i+4t\\,j+2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((2{t}^{2}+1)\\,i+3\\,j+(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-1)\\,k)\\cdot ((3{t}^{2}-1)\\,i+4t\\,j+2t\\,k) \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}+1)(3{t}^{2}-1)+3(4t)+(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-1)(2t) \\\\  & =-1+10t+{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{4}+4{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-1+10t+{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{4}+4{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-t+5{t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}+\\frac{6}{5}{t}^{5}+\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{6}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-1+5+\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{2}{3})-(1+5-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{12}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281556 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c5b07f3-4813-5a4a-a0e6-a5bf77edd48d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278852} {:answer/id #uuid "24161e0a-0b08-5db9-9481-589c38c2931e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278915} {:answer/id #uuid "3927e74a-4b53-5c18-92e5-d51492930769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279093} {:answer/id #uuid "bc856658-afbb-5fe8-bdf2-8d82839c6b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280249} {:answer/id #uuid "f923d8f6-fbaa-5b3e-a1a9-91366f82223e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc856658-afbb-5fe8-bdf2-8d82839c6b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280249} :answer-field/id #uuid "99cbe268-3f0b-5f24-95dd-226717656d00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281106}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "203be68d-096d-564c-bf44-7d62e44d7418" :question/math-academy-id "q-162892" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=2y\\,i-2x\\,j+{e}^{z-2y}\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2y\\,i-2x\\,j+{e}^{z-2y}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+k)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+1\\cdot 2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)-2\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2\\pi +2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-2\\pi +2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[-2\\pi t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-2\\pi (1)+0)-(-2\\pi (-1)+0) \\\\  & =-4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372209 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d45117c8-92b5-5e85-be68-59207cfdcaeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372211} {:answer/id #uuid "94d1fa98-7766-5aa8-ae09-9d3addbd727b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372212} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec03585-7795-522f-a5f2-67e2d22d5768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372213} {:answer/id #uuid "ce20f1dc-7964-554d-81c6-eddb4382c3ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372214} {:answer/id #uuid "fae3e8e3-085f-5a58-b783-baab0dfc6066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce20f1dc-7964-554d-81c6-eddb4382c3ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372214} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4d8f220-0d83-53ee-9569-d72e7c144c7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372210}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "48c5ec06-77b9-5c6d-9d25-1b845d8b6bb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162889" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+k$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=z\\,i+3x\\,j+2{y}^{2}\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z=1.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =z\\,i+3x\\,j+2{y}^{2}\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+0\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+0\\,k) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\frac{3}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{3}{2}t+\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{3}{2}(2\\pi )+0)-(1+\\frac{3}{2}(0)+0) \\\\  & =3\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f568a2f-7b80-5f85-b384-1c1a684d00a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372218} {:answer/id #uuid "db5f892d-b188-5614-98ed-59c650396111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372219} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb21bec-4585-5873-bda2-04c6a3caa80b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372220} {:answer/id #uuid "37824a70-c1a6-564e-97d8-b4dfb038f620" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372221} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2d7d23-39ba-56a6-a28d-95d8896f320a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ceb21bec-4585-5873-bda2-04c6a3caa80b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372220} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e3f2827-69ad-5c26-8f65-1978187ebe9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372217}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "eadc1c42-fade-50b5-8c58-c8e95e3536a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162891" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=(y-z)\\,i+(z-x)\\,j+(x-y)\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(y-z)\\,i+(z-x)\\,j+(x-y)\\,k \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\,i+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,i+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,i+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =-2{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2{sin}^{2}⁡t-2{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2t]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(-1+0-2(2\\pi ))-(-1+0-2(0)) \\\\  & =-4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431185 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a5ee6b0-2be5-5581-a028-5db7075b5455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431148} {:answer/id #uuid "9ceea66b-fcc6-5f70-83a1-36dc6fc02ef6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431161} {:answer/id #uuid "9df165e3-93dc-582f-b6c7-50fa8041ebef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431162} {:answer/id #uuid "b623ffee-6400-53b3-8d80-728480b3c9fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431168} {:answer/id #uuid "f29e0799-e667-5d4c-b0bf-49fdbd64159a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b623ffee-6400-53b3-8d80-728480b3c9fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431168} :answer-field/id #uuid "e70a93bf-0c70-586e-8362-c72402361cae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431180}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54c148d3-85ed-598f-9fe2-1c4177e8c1c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162999" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,j+({t}^{2}-t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=2z\\,i+3y\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t,\\,y={t}^{2}-{t}^{3},\\,z={t}^{2}-t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2z\\,i+3y\\,k \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}-2t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(2t-3{t}^{2})\\,j+(2t-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((2{t}^{2}-2t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3})\\,k)\\cdot ((2t-1)\\,i+(2t-3{t}^{2})\\,j+(2t-1)\\,k) \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot (2t-1)+0\\cdot (2t-3{t}^{2})+(3{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3})\\cdot (2t-1) \\\\  & =(-3{t}^{3}+5{t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot (2t-1) \\\\  & =2t-9{t}^{2}+13{t}^{3}-6{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-9{t}^{2}+13{t}^{3}-6{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3}+\\frac{13}{4}{t}^{4}-\\frac{6}{5}{t}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1-3+\\frac{13}{4}-\\frac{6}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3927b4fa-c581-50e7-9636-601f0c7f2db1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431154} {:answer/id #uuid "a2c0ebdd-99e9-5a8d-ba80-6dbf22ca7b81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431163} {:answer/id #uuid "ad732c00-dd52-57e7-a26e-fd44e8ff888d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431164} {:answer/id #uuid "b206cf8d-5ac9-5e6e-abb0-0881fe9594e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431167} {:answer/id #uuid "e08a22bc-8ede-59c0-a3f4-3fa119eeb7a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b206cf8d-5ac9-5e6e-abb0-0881fe9594e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431167} :answer-field/id #uuid "db93ea83-d9f2-5f0b-8dff-03ea91428db7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431179}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "60b9502a-1750-5d1a-ac6a-f7d7a17af2a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-162314" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,i+(t+{t}^{2})\\,j+(-t-{t}^{2})\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,0).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+x\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}-2t,\\,y=t+{t}^{2},\\,z=-t-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,k \\\\  & =(t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+{t}^{2})\\,j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t-{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(6{t}^{2}-2)\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j+(-1-2t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,k)\\cdot ((6{t}^{2}-2)\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j+(-1-2t)\\,k) \\\\  & =(t+{t}^{2})\\cdot (6{t}^{2}-2)+0\\cdot (1+2t)+(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\cdot (-1-2t) \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}+4{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2{t}^{4}+4{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{t}^{5}}{5}+{t}^{4}+\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-\\frac{2}{5}+1-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Surface Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133929 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133489 :question/id #uuid "03c75b64-65f4-5e12-b7b1-fa8b61893eb7" :question/math-academy-id "e-12447" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=1+x^2+y^2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} = (x+z )\\, \\mathbf{i} + yz \\, \\mathbf{j} + x^2y \\, \\mathbf{k},$ express the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ as a triple integral, and then as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =1+z+0 \\\\  & =z+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R (z+1) \\: \\textrm{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\:-1 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\: y \\leq z \\leq 1+x^2+y^2 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nExpressing our triple integral as a repeated integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z+1\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,z+1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a3fd63f-9991-5781-b4f8-9c4b0abc65ec" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049315 :topic/math-academy-id 4162 :topic/title "Flux Through Closed Surfaces"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050694 :topic/math-academy-id 3361 :topic/title "Green's Theorem in Flux Form"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134135 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffd3c9ec-3cf7-567b-a353-7585d49bb4b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} + z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130967} {:answer/id #uuid "011715b2-f35f-5761-81b5-48d58c2b0ac6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130968} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6bded5-6334-5d42-977c-94cb680710d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} + x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130969} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2e7185-13ca-551c-b2b9-a1f06717c040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y + z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130970} {:answer/id #uuid "7c572ba2-1637-5089-bb75-49b67db7af89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y + x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130971}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7d720396-d9d8-5526-a6c9-d783c2ddfa07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130966}] :question/id #uuid "9360d1cf-9517-569c-8e77-dfd2eb8609da" :question/math-academy-id "q-162407" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x = 1$, $x = 3$, $y =-1$, $y = 1$, $z = 0$, $z = 1$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F = xzi + y^{2}j + k$, the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = \\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{3}\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}A A A A Adzdydx$.\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02b4e90d-c970-54c3-ab06-a30ba09864e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\,z+3y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278607} {:answer/id #uuid "7ebe8543-ec3e-587e-9768-a831766e3eb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}^{y-2z}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279734} {:answer/id #uuid "aa98d390-38c9-5242-9163-03ea126a9877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280105} {:answer/id #uuid "acad9a07-0f5d-5457-86b1-82efec638ac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,2y+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280120} {:answer/id #uuid "ea617dc5-3c2b-587f-bfa4-9046fb77867d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}^{y-2z}\\,2y+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa98d390-38c9-5242-9163-03ea126a9877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280105} :answer-field/id #uuid "26ee8cb1-a891-5ada-b9cf-ae248d766e50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280903}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "152cbc4e-4f16-538e-9423-127439b0fd3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162408" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $x=y-2z$ and $x=4-{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=x{y}^{2}\\,i+xz\\,j+y\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+0+0 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,y-2z\\le x\\le 4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "100640c1-bb74-5261-b07b-162818bcec64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,2xz\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278736} {:answer/id #uuid "612e775d-f4d3-5491-a568-8632b1dc20b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2y}^{2{x}^{2}+1}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279479} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e024e7-b5ac-5532-a30e-fcebd4a57c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2y}^{2{x}^{2}+1}\\,y+z-2\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280046} {:answer/id #uuid "bd3f43b4-3268-56c7-b7af-4bdd5b0709c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,y+z-2\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280255} {:answer/id #uuid "c001c748-078c-5145-9bb6-76a3e6bc31f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c001c748-078c-5145-9bb6-76a3e6bc31f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280282} :answer-field/id #uuid "78ab3a06-7407-5d2e-b3fb-5448f190ff8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281052}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34056bf8-ebf0-50cf-86bc-5d67c31ea40b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162588" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z={x}^{2}+y.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(x+y)\\,i-2y\\,j+{x}^{2}z\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =1+(-2)+{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}({x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le z\\le {x}^{2}+y}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe9f3a4-5cf2-57da-bcd0-f2ef4cb40acb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,x+y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278733} {:answer/id #uuid "22082459-dc16-5814-aa58-32ad4a0572c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278901} {:answer/id #uuid "54ac7f0f-775a-50ab-b1ab-fadfeb9bb310" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}^{2}\\,3{y}^{2}+xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279362} {:answer/id #uuid "5e06d3d8-095d-5f06-bf6e-56940d9ad14d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2}^{{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,3{y}^{2}+xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279452} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f6523c-8035-585f-ad29-1d759e7d0636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}^{2}\\,2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7f6523c-8035-585f-ad29-1d759e7d0636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}^{2}\\,2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280633} :answer-field/id #uuid "070d1c83-7b76-5bd7-a3dc-6b326edc9f9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280842}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e6de64a-d194-59f6-b620-cf3ddbd502d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162589" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and $y=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=({y}^{3}-z)\\,i+z\\,j+2xyz\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xyz) \\\\  & =0+0+2xy \\\\  & =2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type III solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}^{2}\\,2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398562 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a60a0ff-d58d-574d-ad7b-aa68f48f45e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398564} {:answer/id #uuid "caf3aed4-56d3-57fb-9c54-0b9379ad0474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398565} {:answer/id #uuid "5e393df8-940d-5225-8bcc-4aba9a085593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398566} {:answer/id #uuid "8ebc6e76-bfea-5200-aea9-f978a3678ba3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398567} {:answer/id #uuid "c0a5acac-f897-54fc-9f0e-b6da562e322e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+y+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0a5acac-f897-54fc-9f0e-b6da562e322e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+y+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398568} :answer-field/id #uuid "196954bd-17a6-525e-a3ea-36557f290435" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398563}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0d14173-8029-50a6-8f00-d7853c057071" :question/math-academy-id "q-162581" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=0,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=xy\\,i+2y\\,j+{x}^{2}zk,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =y+2+{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+y+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}({x}^{2}+y+2)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,{x}^{2}+y+2\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc53f04c-d5bb-59dd-b389-8e0eefab0229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398571} {:answer/id #uuid "af12ea26-8299-5100-8312-7691bb7b67f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-z+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398572} {:answer/id #uuid "958be202-ad70-5f65-942d-3cf053018cf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398573} {:answer/id #uuid "221c39d4-97a7-58e1-af0f-bd5bd145ae18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x+4z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398574} {:answer/id #uuid "fc84079a-9c1c-54c9-bc3c-5fd874d6a1dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "958be202-ad70-5f65-942d-3cf053018cf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398573} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b4c4f1b-ebdf-554c-9f5f-c8043f8e6124" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398570}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20faa8ac-7475-55a0-94fb-35eb1571259e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162583" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=4,$ $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=({x}^{2}+y)\\,i-yz\\,j+4xz\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4xz) \\\\  & =2x-z+4x \\\\  & =6x-z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(6x-z)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,6x-z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430947 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "499cdd5b-e6c0-5bd4-bfc7-e7acaa3926fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x-z+2}\\,3{z}^{2}-4\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430922} {:answer/id #uuid "4e983312-df08-546e-9806-4e462f4e1659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2}\\,3{z}^{2}-4\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430923} {:answer/id #uuid "688adcb4-8f82-5557-b006-90cbca149a33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2}\\,{z}^{3}-3\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430927} {:answer/id #uuid "8128a8cf-b3f9-556e-aefb-154a9c6699fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x-z+2}\\,{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430931} {:answer/id #uuid "d03cded5-b5e9-5895-a862-9145e719e5f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x-z+2}\\,{z}^{3}-3\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "499cdd5b-e6c0-5bd4-bfc7-e7acaa3926fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x-z+2}\\,3{z}^{2}-4\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430922} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ef02b27-25c8-5d51-9b89-2000755f118a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430943}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00083281-29d7-555a-802b-bb136fd26b95" :question/math-academy-id "q-162586" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=x-z+2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=z\\,i-4y\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{3}) \\\\  & =0-4+3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{z}^{2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}(3{z}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type III solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x-z+2\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(3{z}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x-z+2}\\,3{z}^{2}-4\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430952 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "069804e3-7c58-5f7e-b7b1-a0c2c5c704f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430911} {:answer/id #uuid "06c35222-68d1-50e1-b3be-f3859d1566f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430912} {:answer/id #uuid "165ccd61-a96d-55ca-a818-a06291f0c969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430914} {:answer/id #uuid "661de3a9-92bc-5da3-91fa-17df7bbafc4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430925} {:answer/id #uuid "fd490bcb-15ef-5833-9df8-21e37b4504ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "069804e3-7c58-5f7e-b7b1-a0c2c5c704f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430911} :answer-field/id #uuid "89b39073-1256-589c-ae05-81365bf7916b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430942}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd56ae3f-1187-5181-af79-1866b5188a10" :question/math-academy-id "q-162575" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}\\,i+xyz\\,j+2y\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y) \\\\  & =2x+xz+0 \\\\  & =2x+xz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(2x+xz)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,2x+xz\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Flux Across a Closed Surface as a Triple Integral"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133930 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133490 :question/id #uuid "9bb760a6-9060-532a-a59d-3cedf335daac" :question/math-academy-id "e-12448" :question/problem "Consider the positively oriented surface $S$ given by $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 4$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = 2x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{k}.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4) \\\\  & =2+1+0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $2$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =32\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c239481-1424-575a-b4f7-b6f5409dd240" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048125 :topic/math-academy-id 1144 :topic/title "Volumes of Cylinders"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048997 :topic/math-academy-id 2053 :topic/title "Calculating Volumes of Solids Using Triple Integrals"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049878 :topic/math-academy-id 1753 :topic/title "Volumes of Rectangular Solids"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049953 :topic/math-academy-id 1146 :topic/title "Volumes of Spheres"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c423938-da5c-5986-904d-ca90bf43b04d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278710} {:answer/id #uuid "1603c0df-af5e-5636-b1a6-72475e40234a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278787} {:answer/id #uuid "1d02c2fc-86f7-5eda-b6b6-62da495ea6ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\,000\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278860} {:answer/id #uuid "4b1ed5c0-e3de-599b-b026-5cb574362292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{400\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279264} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc2db2a-7618-540a-a2a1-b6f665e7242c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{800\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d02c2fc-86f7-5eda-b6b6-62da495ea6ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\,000\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278860} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9ce83a8-eea0-5bfa-a466-b577953f924a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281161}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "088370e5-5be7-5c78-9dd1-180055943e46" :question/math-academy-id "q-162410" :question/problem "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=25$ and the vector field $F=x\\,i+xz\\,j+3z\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3z) \\\\  & =1+0+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $5$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {5}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\,000\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d80dd4e3-7ed1-503d-9671-7f45280ca24f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130985} {:answer/id #uuid "17a086d8-d007-5821-8115-e76ddac0915a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130986} {:answer/id #uuid "4a19f710-b32c-5035-a13b-72785acbdf61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130987} {:answer/id #uuid "67b10b1f-2e11-5777-9ba4-8b1485780761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130988} {:answer/id #uuid "755be662-4415-5c52-965a-228af18f717c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130989}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4a64fc7d-ca43-526d-a9de-b6748f05a24c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130984} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60179c77-308c-5a4b-9b41-da233c3efd4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374181} {:answer/id #uuid "c11f9a42-43be-517a-94cc-42c6ba595526" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374182} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d48b0f-7937-5ff0-8ff5-b0a889036dee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374183} {:answer/id #uuid "ae328f95-387f-55bc-b6b1-78da1a0e6c8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374184} {:answer/id #uuid "9829f33d-f493-59a1-b8be-a91cbaec38dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60179c77-308c-5a4b-9b41-da233c3efd4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374181} :answer-field/id #uuid "a29f5580-8460-5ca2-bf68-68db1fc8ae7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374180}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de14125f-fde2-50fc-9770-aae06e6d9c30" :question/math-academy-id "q-162411" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0f6f5ed91981e15d2fb80e2630cec6d20f566feb627899f529e07bab12b73fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨{z}^{2},\\,2y,\\,{x}^{2}+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =0+2+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid cylinder enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a529250-7d38-56c1-a622-ff64fed6a943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278829} {:answer/id #uuid "2f3cea9e-711c-52bd-bf9d-eaae4c5bf127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279003} {:answer/id #uuid "417909d6-a428-5bad-93a3-7802edb9f815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279176} {:answer/id #uuid "520a773b-54ab-554d-965c-034440c2452d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279338} {:answer/id #uuid "9219b018-274a-5760-bb2c-8a5c016a3ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f3cea9e-711c-52bd-bf9d-eaae4c5bf127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279003} :answer-field/id #uuid "c807b5db-45e6-5011-a1a3-2596f4f3033b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281182}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c0ba9cf-530e-5fff-bddf-77afd0f80ef9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162569" :question/problem "![](images/58/581ac462bfcfead22a6c5ad4eb247f05adb1a677e92cac51954b0746cd083ab7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨x-y,\\,y+z,\\,2x-3z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2x-3z) \\\\  & =1+1+(-3) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid cylinder enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}-1\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =-{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =-\\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =-\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-12\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281500 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d047b1e-3afb-5c5a-88f9-faeab4432ef1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279129} {:answer/id #uuid "565cbac4-9d5b-53f7-ad95-f3c76b6932d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279384} {:answer/id #uuid "93da9f2a-8ea0-53d4-858e-491166b1dc48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279908} {:answer/id #uuid "a5861e93-47f0-536a-a4e4-9d3f7a9b658a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280057} {:answer/id #uuid "da4056b9-a344-5551-a7d2-55af24530e22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d047b1e-3afb-5c5a-88f9-faeab4432ef1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279129} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b030670-12c0-5a90-a682-f7582e669ba1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280989}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c50889a1-249a-5104-a142-d8d313afba10" :question/math-academy-id "q-162572" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aac27058402f89bef73700129a7812b3cf4f971c11975fc1711a46b8d10e61fe.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨3x+y{e}^{z},\\,xy+2,\\,1-xz⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x+y{e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+2)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(1-xz) \\\\  & =3+x+(-x) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the rectangular solid enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3274b94e-9050-5914-92cf-a20e7f33b9f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279033} {:answer/id #uuid "4021cef0-295e-5798-9f4f-2157ebca2b8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{128\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279158} {:answer/id #uuid "59d4c631-de3a-5f80-abfd-ae0e4830d3d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{256\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279414} {:answer/id #uuid "659a3952-f331-5c0f-9621-9a4212215836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279515} {:answer/id #uuid "a356390a-20fd-5203-b264-de87732ef03b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "659a3952-f331-5c0f-9621-9a4212215836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279515} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f6afff7-29bd-5084-9d8e-63605244a213" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280961}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9b12219-fd15-591a-8410-7eb56a488ff2" :question/math-academy-id "q-162578" :question/problem "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z-4)^{2}=4$ and the vector field $F=z\\,i+6y\\,j+(x+3z)\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+3z) \\\\  & =0+6+3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $2$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}9\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =9{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =96\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dd0be77-03d3-5682-acf0-3a7644fad46d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278994} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4cf883-7b99-518c-bfbc-917db8f1e538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279587} {:answer/id #uuid "b9ca94f3-a2e6-5cf7-9f7b-6d1fec661b99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280228} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b1f74d-c55b-5f6c-8668-cf5d8fcbf163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280442} {:answer/id #uuid "e434d311-7872-56cf-97d6-9851e8e02577" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e434d311-7872-56cf-97d6-9851e8e02577" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280605} :answer-field/id #uuid "70df89b8-d119-5821-aca7-6b92f8aab9b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281032}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a625adb-c2bb-5fe3-8ab2-cff87a075be0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162580" :question/problem "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by $(x+2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ and the vector field $F=({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,i+4y\\,j+2xz\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xz) \\\\  & =-2x+4+2x \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $1$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dedb4d6-dae2-543b-80b2-9ccef9fa5a88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374188} {:answer/id #uuid "8eabaa4a-a54f-5ddc-885b-ce8690b34285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374189} {:answer/id #uuid "896566d9-4a61-5d61-b4f4-4aa45c0939f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374190} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc850a0-843d-56eb-abc2-2419b6e13be9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374191} {:answer/id #uuid "af363662-dfd8-5c09-8594-73404f30ea3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8eabaa4a-a54f-5ddc-885b-ce8690b34285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374189} :answer-field/id #uuid "9accbfc9-5746-540c-8cdb-b27e56facfc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374187}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "139943e3-1c42-5e1e-8135-8c3ce7b2dffc" :question/math-academy-id "q-162570" :question/problem "![](images/98/9861e9edc3c7517af6fd912046f62ec04a6e478942c3017ed9903e8b366130c2.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨1-2x,\\,5y-3{z}^{2},\\,{x}^{3}-{y}^{2}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5y-3{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{3}-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2+5+0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid cylinder enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =54\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374193 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a718cce9-8e5d-5dec-8a0a-3840ae5b697b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374195} {:answer/id #uuid "b2dec189-d251-5cdb-beb7-7e7b56595d20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374196} {:answer/id #uuid "1af109f7-1025-5d70-baae-5d18a7702281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374197} {:answer/id #uuid "a2591ccc-8c08-599f-b2a7-b656cee9d9f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374198} {:answer/id #uuid "0653f4e3-f779-564a-8100-ee0e0831159c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2dec189-d251-5cdb-beb7-7e7b56595d20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374196} :answer-field/id #uuid "24fbd410-95a8-5da8-9827-3827f59a3a95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374194}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a61b4d5a-a480-5473-9f9f-c9865eee4653" :question/math-academy-id "q-162571" :question/problem "![](images/07/07b46ab683e54813e0f6090c6841302ea7d914bc6b1577afc883f6e42e0a3903.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨3x-y{z}^{2},\\,y+{e}^{z},\\,2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-y{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y+{e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =3+1+0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the rectangular solid enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19f3436d-bc76-5261-8ad1-368ca3b77f95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430915} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6b3f5a-f220-5554-9220-850158e49738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430919} {:answer/id #uuid "6b730726-fed2-5b3e-8ffc-354b2c3bf2f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430929} {:answer/id #uuid "e4005834-c2ac-5578-8587-1d569f479bce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430936} {:answer/id #uuid "e993ee67-8cbb-5ee4-a9bb-9dc04ab4bbe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b730726-fed2-5b3e-8ffc-354b2c3bf2f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430929} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4a0d219-93b4-5cee-98eb-dc3219549951" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430945}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28a83ce5-6b28-57bd-96e8-b831ae6041a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162573" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6dc4f6d9e8520dead79789efd4f08c07ea7921d28f4cf6f8da06de533d012e90.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨y-z,\\,y-2x,\\,x+4z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+4z) \\\\  & =0+1+4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the rectangular solid enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}5\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =5{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b92e2ac-43e0-56d7-9ecd-75553160a01c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430918} {:answer/id #uuid "41e506e3-ebbe-5f8b-ae29-c18276419687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430920} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7901d4-d410-5e21-8e33-71572db7a730" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430924} {:answer/id #uuid "6aacb83d-c030-5c62-a7af-140b5f553083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430928} {:answer/id #uuid "ceca5263-40f9-5ba9-8496-31c6fead0bd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41e506e3-ebbe-5f8b-ae29-c18276419687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430920} :answer-field/id #uuid "b15f2475-f304-5fb8-a434-536b4a248735" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430944}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f2b40e8-b423-5c7a-b959-43a67763f792" :question/math-academy-id "q-162574" :question/problem "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ and the vector field $F=(yz-2x)\\,i+3y\\,j+(x+z)\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz-2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+z) \\\\  & =-2+3+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $3$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{3} \\\\  & =72\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across the Surface of a Known Solid"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133931 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133491 :question/id #uuid "dfba5156-6b5f-5622-9406-7598ae2630b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12449" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} =e^z \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2y^2 \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4x^2yz \\, \\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{x}^{2}yz) \\\\  & =0+2{x}^{2}y+4{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfbacc2c-3295-5427-aa6d-abb9d46ca58b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130991} {:answer/id #uuid "068f8b0d-155c-5288-ab99-7bef355ce630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130992} {:answer/id #uuid "217bf025-09a6-5388-b75e-9b4aa6c0931a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130993} {:answer/id #uuid "3f8b540c-c595-5796-b0a9-c06e4f0ff6a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130994} {:answer/id #uuid "4935e6a8-5d04-5659-bd19-c0da643e24ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130995}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3cf5d99a-43d2-5f4d-9cab-2d45271876ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130990}] :question/id #uuid "c4d6f3d3-e8a0-54e3-96bf-06ac782d45a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162412" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x =-1$, $x = 1$, $y = 0$, $y = 1$, $z = 0$, $z = 1$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F = 2xzi + (x^{2} + z)j + 2k$, find the flux of $F$ across $S$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "305d1aaf-bbea-5c5f-a549-96aeb913edda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279014} {:answer/id #uuid "7052a4ed-9911-5fd7-bc04-95f5aa7e4565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279610} {:answer/id #uuid "774d71e4-5b4e-524f-b577-edc0dd3ee10b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279657} {:answer/id #uuid "ca898aae-6598-59af-88de-b8842924f741" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280367} {:answer/id #uuid "cd52ceb4-0135-536f-8588-e294bafcb89b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd52ceb4-0135-536f-8588-e294bafcb89b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280401} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6048b0a-8782-5f08-a33d-6088d1e77789" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281271}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b54a2292-f496-593a-8f3d-c0762d77f082" :question/math-academy-id "q-162606" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,j+(x+y)\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+y) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+0+0 \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡z]_{z=0}^{z=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =({2}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot 1\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235537 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "638bdd3c-988f-5b06-9b5e-88c81e7acaff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "638bdd3c-988f-5b06-9b5e-88c81e7acaff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234699} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9aaca52-991c-54fa-a8a1-2c32329e23f6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235219}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7dc47c56-52c7-5494-aceb-b1baa61e89b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-168617" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the region. The flux of the vector field $F$ across $S$ represents the rate at which the air passes through the surface. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to write down the integral that gives this rate.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required rate is ${∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nIn part 1, we found that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}2x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "082e3c86-e949-5ff4-b33f-cb02c016d1d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278667} {:answer/id #uuid "149426fb-57b1-536b-9c11-dd047080a433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278775} {:answer/id #uuid "16d8f309-fda3-575c-a9d3-a8ec0f44cdcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278793} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae65590-f27f-55dc-9769-e7944f343866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279261} {:answer/id #uuid "9c741780-eba9-5519-8797-113033813b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c741780-eba9-5519-8797-113033813b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279984} :answer-field/id #uuid "af809870-df22-50bf-a671-64310449b9b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281137}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e758b54-bd1f-5329-88cb-61523affaf73" :question/math-academy-id "q-162594" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=4,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}y\\,i+2z\\,j+xyz\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xyz) \\\\  & =2xy+0+xy \\\\  & =3xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3xy\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}3xy\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}3\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=4}\\cdot [3z]_{z=1}^{z=2} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (8-0)\\cdot (6-3) \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281506 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52186257-9f4b-56fb-b960-29a2f63ea399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279339} {:answer/id #uuid "697dee97-f93f-5355-b963-f4f2ed4536b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279547} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c9b24c-57c8-5afa-a32e-082d761b5954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280164} {:answer/id #uuid "e0aaa464-a730-5cbb-ab6f-fdbf65421878" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280578} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf8c058-83e0-502b-802f-07d67539564b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "697dee97-f93f-5355-b963-f4f2ed4536b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279547} :answer-field/id #uuid "12ac0596-1f70-519e-8661-fc2d524e2e33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280863}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dea24fde-a211-5010-b92d-b13afeca9f75" :question/math-academy-id "q-162596" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=1,$ $x=e,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,i+{z}^{2}\\,j+(x-{y}^{3})\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x-{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+0+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{x=1}^{x=e}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\cdot [z]_{z=0}^{z=2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\cdot (\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2})\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281507 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "453e572a-9b7a-5c8b-af0a-d4bbab3e02c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279211} {:answer/id #uuid "719c2ade-ec2a-5fc4-81bc-42bee8f852aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279622} {:answer/id #uuid "a8b4c658-0c6e-58c5-a593-f19bdd138a00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280083} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e4f34b-b03f-5102-8a05-51e7755ec9a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280208} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7e88cc-1b76-537b-a6f1-169efb2d510e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8b4c658-0c6e-58c5-a593-f19bdd138a00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280083} :answer-field/id #uuid "e81d88b2-55dd-5e26-8ccb-24f90e955d12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281246}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7cc5e4b-aa70-5d55-82a4-140961e3cc36" :question/math-academy-id "q-162599" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=3.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}\\,i+2y\\,j+(x+{y}^{2})\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2x+2+0 \\\\  & =2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(2x+2)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(2x+2)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}+2x]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}+2(1)-0)\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0828f14c-57b5-5bff-96a4-41f2ecf9c8cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278666} {:answer/id #uuid "601888a6-bae8-5265-8032-ca7466880974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279474} {:answer/id #uuid "6f899f87-fc79-53d1-9d8b-59f00b8892d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279603} {:answer/id #uuid "ce7b1ce6-5db6-5b72-8254-8b233c23921a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280411} {:answer/id #uuid "f34738d0-7faa-5814-82e1-6d902521196c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f899f87-fc79-53d1-9d8b-59f00b8892d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279603} :answer-field/id #uuid "22288074-59ca-5f02-9943-023604b9357b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280889}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a55db798-fcb0-54c3-9d76-3d7d1e841ffa" :question/math-academy-id "q-162600" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=-1,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=2y\\,i+x{y}^{2}\\,j+2xyz\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xyz) \\\\  & =0+2xy+2xy \\\\  & =4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4xy\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}4xy\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=2}\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (4-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25469fa9-aa9b-5ce2-912f-092bcf0c9271" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374202} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee396c4-83e0-5fa2-ae23-69048563ecb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374203} {:answer/id #uuid "ddac6d93-fada-5b42-9d98-dffa0367488c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374204} {:answer/id #uuid "c8406793-5096-5aff-b557-a3bebc57cf38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374205} {:answer/id #uuid "88de07a7-1a0f-5f7b-b1a1-718b8c6821c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddac6d93-fada-5b42-9d98-dffa0367488c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374204} :answer-field/id #uuid "940992af-dba8-51a2-a024-f36c67d58c17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374201}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1604058-cdd6-524d-a2be-ab81c147d2ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-162597" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=1,$ $x=3,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=-1,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(y-{x}^{2})\\,i+xy\\,j+3xz\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3xz) \\\\  & =(-2x)+x+3x \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=1}^{x=3}\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(9-1)\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374207 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a1a9148-8e77-5639-ad7f-cbca7f0df0cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374209} {:answer/id #uuid "0bcc0510-4039-55e0-823c-e4ac7d5653d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374210} {:answer/id #uuid "0acfab67-9718-5e64-b967-a1914c71eddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374211} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf62725-3d23-5464-bf59-4f18a980c4db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374212} {:answer/id #uuid "702faaa4-c282-5398-860f-c6e1f3c9bb1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a1a9148-8e77-5639-ad7f-cbca7f0df0cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374209} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ced5422-0477-54ea-a063-de1490b4e5d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374208}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2a8924c-323c-54ea-b830-8522b9835da1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162602" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=3,$ $z=1,$ $z=3.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(2{x}^{3}+z)\\,i-z\\,j-3{x}^{2}z\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-3{x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+0+(-3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=-1}^{x=1}\\cdot (3-0)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3})\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430948 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12f5be7e-0bfc-59a7-af3a-6dfcf5d7d844" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430913} {:answer/id #uuid "23e226ce-01b7-5120-bfc5-5949d300bfcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430916} {:answer/id #uuid "475b50f4-a66e-582c-bb09-b18513917dc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430921} {:answer/id #uuid "e03f06d7-09f6-5991-8022-a31e044488bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430935} {:answer/id #uuid "ecbcc9d3-a1f4-5e44-b6a6-513ff6a3a28b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecbcc9d3-a1f4-5e44-b6a6-513ff6a3a28b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430938} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc27d6bf-860e-505c-82f5-df4b985c7b2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430946}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09c354f4-ffe0-5d80-b23d-97b7671b8b06" :question/math-academy-id "q-162595" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=-1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=y{z}^{2}\\,i-2yz\\,j+3{z}^{2}\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =0+(-2z)+6z \\\\  & =4z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4z\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{2}4z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =(2-(-1))\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [2{z}^{2}]_{z=-1}^{z=2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot (8-2) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430950 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24ea0e2b-5e7a-52c9-a1b3-f61827c82bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430917} {:answer/id #uuid "676e7034-5bd6-59ee-bc64-fafa0a12a224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430926} {:answer/id #uuid "727b00ee-627c-530d-87da-873160a2d3d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430930} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5308bb-1224-54c9-a249-060024986a6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430934} {:answer/id #uuid "fa91438f-d562-5478-84cb-9cc0479a64c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "727b00ee-627c-530d-87da-873160a2d3d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430930} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f84ddbb-0ce0-5549-8bb9-1dfef0b987f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430941}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ce23f40-f256-54cd-9b13-888ae9d3dff9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162605" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=3,$ $z=0,$ $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(y+{z}^{2})\\,i-x\\,j+3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y+{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =0+0+3{x}^{2}{e}^{z} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot (3-1)\\cdot [{e}^{z}]_{z=0}^{z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =({1}^{3}-{0}^{3})\\cdot 2\\cdot ({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}]}
